

Methods

1. System Overview

The Supernumeracy Species Simulation Engine integrates encrypted astrophysical data ingestion, multi‑species manifold evolution, GPU‑accelerated PDE/ODE solvers, and stellar coupling overlays. All computation is performed on ciphertext fields using homomorphic encryption to preserve reversibility and auditability.

The methods below describe the full computational pipeline, including data preparation, mathematical operators, numerical solvers, GPU kernel architecture, and validation procedures.

2. Data Ingestion & Encryption

2.1 Astrophysical Dataset Acquisition

Real astrophysical datasets are sourced from external observatories and machine‑curated pipelines (AstroMachine). Data types include:

Stellar density fields

Spectral energy distributions

Gas/particle distribution maps

Temporal evolution snapshots

2.2 Encryption Pipeline

All incoming data is encrypted using AES‑GCM‑256 with homomorphic extensions. The encryption operator (E) maps plaintext fields (x) to ciphertext fields (E(x)) while preserving polynomial operations.

Key components:

Post‑quantum key management

Encrypted database storage

Homomorphic gate compatibility

This ensures the simulation operates entirely on encrypted data.

3. Species Definition & Initialization

3.1 Species Definition Matrix

The system defines (N = 100) species fields. Each species (s_i) is represented as a scalar density function over a spatial grid:

[ s_i : \Omega \times \mathbb{R}^+ \rightarrow \mathbb{R} ]

3.2 Interaction Graph Construction

Species interactions are encoded in a 100×100 adjacency matrix (A):

[ A_{ij} \in \mathbb{R} ]

Edges represent:

cooperative interactions

competitive interactions

predator–prey dynamics

oscillatory couplings

The graph is sparse to ensure computational efficiency.

4. Governing Equations

4.1 PDE/ODE Dynamics

Species evolution is governed by:

[ \frac{d s_i}{dt} = D_i \nabla^2 s_i + \sum_{j=1}^{100} A_{ij} F_{ij}(s_i, s_j) + G_i(s_i, \nabla s_i, t) ]

Where:

(D_i): diffusion coefficient

(F_{ij}): pairwise interaction function

(G_i): self‑dynamics

4.2 Interaction Functions

Supported interaction functions include:

multiplicative coupling

predator–prey dynamics

competitive exclusion

oscillatory resonance

These functions are polynomial‑expressible, enabling homomorphic evaluation.

5. Numerical Methods

5.1 Spatial Discretization

The spatial domain (\Omega) is discretized into a uniform grid (128×128 minimum). Laplacian operators use a 5‑point or 9‑point stencil depending on precision requirements.

5.2 Temporal Integration

Time evolution uses:

Euler method (baseline)

Runge–Kutta 4 (high‑precision mode)

All integrators operate on encrypted fields.

5.3 Stability Controls

To ensure numerical stability:

CFL condition checks

adaptive timestep selection

diffusion‑dominant stabilization

encrypted error monitoring

6. GPU Kernel Architecture

6.1 Kernel Classes

Six kernel classes operate in parallel:

Diffusion Kernel

Interaction Kernel

Self‑Dynamics Kernel

PDE/ODE Integrator Kernel

Stellar Coupling Kernel

Homomorphic Gate Kernel

Each kernel processes encrypted tensors using CUDA/ROCm.

6.2 Parallelization Strategy

Species fields are updated in parallel across GPU threads. Interaction terms use shared memory for adjacency matrix access. Encryption gates are evaluated using polynomial approximations compatible with ciphertext operations.

7. Stellar Coupling Methods

7.1 Astrophysical Overlay

Stellar evolution models ingest species fields to compute:

density overlays

resonance patterns

